        <briefdescription>
<para>Constructor. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal"><ref refid="classostk_1_1astrodynamics_1_1conjunction_1_1probabilityofcollisionalgorithm_1_1_foster1992_1aa29fdcfe7ae96210997e905c86e6fe44" kindref="member">Foster1992</ref><sp/>algorithm<sp/>=<sp/>{}<sp/>;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><ref refid="classostk_1_1astrodynamics_1_1conjunction_1_1probabilityofcollisionalgorithm_1_1_foster1992_1aa29fdcfe7ae96210997e905c86e6fe44" kindref="member">Foster1992</ref><sp/>algorithm<sp/>=<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>Derived(100.0,<sp/>Derived::Unit::MeterPerSecond()),<sp/>Frame::GCRF(),<sp/>Angle::Degrees(0.5),<sp/>12,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="keyword">true</highlight><highlight class="normal"></highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">}<sp/>;</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para><simplesect kind="note"><para>The default step sizes are those of Foster&apos;s numerical implementation, as reported by Alfano (2007): &quot;the
angle theta step size is 0.5 deg, and the radius r step size is OBJ/12&quot; (OBJ being the combined hard-body radius). Alfano (2007) also notes that the accuracy degrades when the combined hard-body radius is smaller than the miss distance but larger than the smaller standard deviation, which smaller step sizes can remedy.</para>
</simplesect>
<parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>aRelativeSpeedThreshold</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>A relative speed threshold below which the close approach is considered low relative speed and the algorithm is not applicable. Defaults to 100 m/s </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>aFrameSPtr</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>An inertial (or quasi-inertial) frame to use. Defaults to Frame::GCRF() </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>anAngularStepSize</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>An angular step size of the polar integration, within (0, 360] deg. If it does not divide a full turn evenly, the number of angular slices is rounded up (and the step size reduced accordingly). Defaults to 0.5 deg </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>aNumberOfRadialSteps</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>A number of radial steps of the polar integration (i.e. the radial step size is the combined hard-body radius divided by this number), strictly positive. Defaults to 12 </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>increaseNumberOfRadialStepsIfNeeded</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Whether to increase the number of radial steps (i.e. reduce the radial step size) if needed so that the smaller standard deviation also spans at least the given number of radial steps, so that the probability density function is resolved when the combined hard-body radius is large compared to the smaller standard deviation. This avoids the above-mentioned accuracy degradation at the expense of a slower computation. However, this type of scenarios are rare in practice. Defaults to true </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>
